# Centered original-domain Poincare: independent source preread

Reviewer: `phase_source_reviewer_20261005`; 2026-10-06. This is a primary-first source/API prerequisite audit and an authored route assessment. It admits no theorem, prototype, SAU, Poincare certificate or verification state. No compiler ran. The original primary contract was written before the committed proposal was read. Future ignored prototypes, decoder reconstructions and mathematics verdicts were excluded. Earlier independent residual/gradient-domain reviews and earlier disclosed covariance signature/keyword exposure remain part of reviewer history; this is not a claim of complete historical blindness.

The v1 HTML was reopened directly on this date. There is no local primary HTML file or invented source-file hash. PBPS anchors: [§2.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#S2.SS2), HTML 168–212; [Appendix C.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1), 1197–1237; [Appendix D.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A4.SS2), 1601–1630. Global source assumptions are at 98–100. SPHMC: [§4.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1), 403–430, with normalized curvature convention at 196.

PBPS uses C² V, 0<α≤β, αI≤Hess V≤βI, and 0<η≤1/β. Its unreflected conditional has potential V(x)+‖x−y‖²/(2η); C.1 uses its reflected pushforward u=2x−y. C.1 applies conditional Poincare to the unnormalized parameter-y score, then covariance Cauchy–Schwarz. D.6 states scalar Poincare from lower curvature; D.9 states two-sided covariance with both curvatures. These sources invoke background analysis and do not print the proposed resolvent construction. SPHMC (4.1) expresses the smoothed Hessian using RGO covariance; Lemma 4.1 uses covariance upper and lower bounds separately. Its β=1 normalization yields a distinct unreflected scale.

## Seven-slot contract for the proposed integration

| Slot | Exact proposed boundary |
|---|---|
| Objects | Actual probability μ=volume.tilted(−W), original compact-smooth gradient D, A=D.closure, actual centered z in A.domain. Conditional instance is the already constructed common J/R/S and S_y, with W_y(u)=V((y+u)/2)+‖u−y‖²/(8η). |
| Domains | Real weighted L² classes and their AE representatives; graph closure of that original D. No ordinary-volume variance, supplied Poincare, replacement operator, adjoint identity or generator-core premise. Noncompact scores enter through their actual C¹/function-and-gradient L² graph producer. |
| Quantifiers | Common J/R/S → every y → one original D_y → all centered domain z and all score directions. For fixed z, ε_n>0→0 → actual u_n in the same A.domain → all variational tests. The operator is fixed before inputs; no jointly measurable fiberwise selection is provided. |
| Assumptions | Generic route: finite-dimensional real Hilbert/Borel E, including zero dimension; C² W, genuine integrable exp(−W), positive Z, exact normalized law; 0<m≤M, both global Hessian bounds, exact closable compact-smooth D. Source α/β and η cap supply these stronger generic premises. |
| Conclusion | Proposed centered-domain estimate m‖z‖²≤‖Az‖²; then an actual normalized variance/gradient-energy corollary for graph-admissible functions and the actual directional score. No ε=0 solution is necessary. |
| Scope | Authored omitted analytic prerequisite toward source conditional Poincare. It would not prove the full lower-curvature-only D.6 statement, anisotropic BL, normalized conditional-density differentiation (C.1), marginal Poincare, core/range equivalence or either main result. |
| Constants | Reflected m=(α+η⁻¹)/4, M=(β+η⁻¹)/4; c=(η⁻¹−α)/4≥0. Unreflected RGO m₀=α+η⁻¹, M₀=β+η⁻¹. Never transfer quarter constants without the actual affine adapter. |

## Actual API inventory and shortest route (seven steps)

1. `GlobalWeightedResolventCoercivity.global_weak_resolvent_coercivity` (464–517) constructs an actual positive-ε variational u in this A.domain, with m‖Au‖²≤‖z−εu‖²≤‖z‖². It consumes both Hessian bounds, not a final coercivity certificate. `CenteredGibbsResolventLimit.gibbs_centered_resolvent_sequence` (11–35) independently constructs actual u_n and proves ε_nu_n→0 for genuinely mean-zero forcing, retaining ordinary local weak-gradient facts. Its C¹ law interface is weaker than the coercivity producer's C² hypothesis; use the latter for this integration.

2. Set ε_n=1/(n+1). Prove positivity and the actual filter limit. Identify the two witnesses for each n through `ClosedGraphResolvent.weak_resolvent` (24–34): its returned uniqueness applies to all variational solutions in the same closed partial operator domain. Equivalently subtract equations and test their difference. Equality of existential choices cannot be assumed. No mean-zero u_n or uniform unscaled ‖u_n‖ bound is required.

3. Test the identified variational equation against z. Cauchy–Schwarz and m>0 give

   ‖z‖² = ⟨ε_nu_n,z⟩+⟨Au_n,Az⟩,
   √m‖z‖² ≤ √m⟨ε_nu_n,z⟩+‖z‖‖Az‖.

   Pass the genuine scalar residual limit, split ‖z‖=0 before cancellation, and square nonnegative quantities. This is a finite scalar argument, not ε=0 range passage or a supplied Poincare norm bound.

4. For an actual graph-admissible q, derive L¹ from L² and probability. Use `WeightedC1GradientDomain.constants_in_closed_gradient` (223–243) to obtain the same-domain constant c_q=∫q dμ. Subtract this domain element from the actual q element; closed partial-map linearity gives its unchanged gradient and probability normalization gives mean zero. Existing `Poincare.Admissible` only states three integrability facts; `Poincare.variance_le` consumes an inequality and cannot produce it.

5. Prove the AE quotient identities explicitly: ‖q−c_q‖²=∫(q−∫q)²dμ and ‖Aq‖²=∫‖gradient q‖²dμ. Pinned Mathlib `L2.inner_def` and `real_inner_self_eq_norm_sq`, plus `MemLp.toLp` AE identities, suffice as relevant APIs. Subtraction, square and inner products require real integrability; totalized integrals are not evidence. These adapters must still be implemented rather than left as reader prose.

6. `ConditionalScoreClosedDomain.conditional_score_closed_gradient_domain` (14–44) already fixes common J/R/S and the same original D_y before every constant, domain element and direction; it produces q_a(u)=s_y(u)[a], its C¹/L² graph pair and pointwise ‖gradient q_a‖≤c‖a‖. `ConditionalScoreDomain` gives the true Hessian formula, so the missing public upper W_y bound is derivable from the original β premise, with coefficient M. Combining the proposed variance estimate and actual probability mass gives c²/m times ‖a‖², namely (η⁻¹−α)²‖a‖²/[4(α+η⁻¹)]. Center q_a by its actual mean; q_a itself is not assumed centered. Auxiliary S2_y may be transferred only by its literal tilted-law equality; this does not identify R2 with R or permit a new D.

7. A later unreflected linear-observable covariance consumer needs actual L² position/linear tests, same-domain graph membership, covariance/variance identification and the correct m₀. For SPHMC α=κ⁻¹, β=1, this would give covariance upper (κ⁻¹+η⁻¹)⁻¹ and then Hess V_η lower 1/(κ+η), only after the actual posterior/Hessian identity and normalization adapter are connected. It is separate from the already distinct covariance-lower/Cramér–Rao half and from anisotropic inverse-Hessian BL.

## Failure boundaries and hidden contracts

The narrow route is mathematically coherent as a proposal. Its concrete missing nodes are witness equality and scalar limiting/cancellation, then centered quotient/domain and integral variance/energy adapters, then the actual conditional score consumer. There is no future Lean proof review here. A generic theorem with finite global M must disclose its extra premise relative to lower-curvature-only D.6; the actual two companion models provide it. Ordinary local-PDE data do not by themselves constitute the weighted mean/energy adapters.

Positive Z and the actual normalized probability are indispensable; no extra position moment, C∞ potential, global bounded drift, or mean-zero resolvent solution is introduced. For E of dimension zero all scalar classes are constants, centered z is zero, and every direction a is zero; avoid a nonexistent unit direction or division by its norm. The arbitrary-a score estimate gives the appropriate trivial specialization. Do not identify reflected and unreflected laws, operators, energies or kernels without a true pushforward/Jacobian map. C.1's normalized conditional differentiation, integrated outer-y covariance bound, marginal spectral argument, full BL/Poincare program, ε=0 range/core and sampler conclusions remain separate.

